description:
I'm able to reproduce a valgrind error when running a test in one of my packages. The package requires compilation, but removing the function from `bench` that the test uses results in a pass when checked with `--with-valgrind`. The Rcpp function in my package is very simple and I'm 99% confident it has no memory leak.

The relevant test is/was at:

https://github.com/HughParsonage/hutilscpp/blob/57a91eb29ab19ce79e3e530e879c2b1ceab1bb4e/tests/testthat/test-bench_system_time.R#L1

which causes the following valgrind error:

```
   Last 13 lines of output:
     ==2566==     in use at exit: 243,482,913 bytes in 15,679 blocks
     ==2566==   total heap usage: 218,068 allocs, 202,389 frees, 7,704,349,228 bytes allocated
     ==2566== 
     ==2566== LEAK SUMMARY:
     ==2566==    definitely lost: 0 bytes in 0 blocks
     ==2566==    indirectly lost: 0 bytes in 0 blocks
     ==2566==      possibly lost: 912 bytes in 3 blocks
     ==2566==    still reachable: 243,482,001 bytes in 15,676 blocks
     ==2566==                       of which reachable via heuristic:
     ==2566==                         newarray           : 4,264 bytes in 1 blocks
     ==2566==         suppressed: 0 bytes in 0 blocks
     ==2566== Rerun with --leak-check=full to see details of leaked memory
     ==2566== 
     ==2566== For counts of detected and suppressed errors, rerun with: -v
     ==2566== ERROR SUMMARY: 0 errors from 0 contexts (suppressed: 0 from 0)
```

```r
context("test-bench_system_time")

test_that("bench_system_time covg", {
  skip_if_not_installed("bench")
  expr <- x <- rep_len(c(0, -0.5, 1.2), 5e6)
  bench_x <- bench_system_time(pminC(x, 0))
  expect_lt(as.numeric(bench_x)[2], 1)
})
```

The definitions of `bench_system_time` and `pminC` are:

```r
bench_system_time <- function(expr) {
  # bench::system_time only evaluates expr in the
  # parent environment (reasonably). So we copy
  # the names of everything in expr so that
  # bench::system_time can run.
  for (`***` in all.names(substitute(expr))) {
    assign(`***`, value = get(`***`, envir = parent.frame()))
  }
  if (requireNamespace("bench", quietly = TRUE)) {
    bench::system_time(expr)
  }
}
```

and `pminC`

```r
pminC <- function(x, a = 0, in_place = FALSE) {
  if (!is.numeric(x)) {
    stop("`x` was a ", class(x), ", but must be numeric.")
  }
  if (!length(x)) {
    return(x)
  }
  if (!is.numeric(a)) {
    stop("`a` was a ", class(a), ", but must be numeric.")
  }
  if (length(a) != 1L) {
    stop("`a` had length ", length(a), ", but must be length-one. ",
         "If you require the parallel maximum of two equal-length vectors, ",
         "use pmaxV(x, y).")
  }
  check_TF(in_place)
  do_pminC(x, a, in_place = in_place)
}
```

```cpp
// [[Rcpp::export]]
NumericVector do_pminC(NumericVector x, double a, bool in_place = false) {
  int n = x.length();
  NumericVector out = in_place ? NumericVector(x) : NumericVector(clone(x));

  for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
    double xi = x[i];
    if (xi > a) {
      out[i] = a;
    }
  }

  return out;
}
```

I'll admit my use of `bench_system_time` is a bit weird (I only use it so I don't have to write `requireNamespace("bench", quietly = TRUE)` in every example), but thought I would flag nonetheless.

Using something like this:

```R
count_gc <- function() {
  complete <- FALSE
  reg.finalizer(environment(), function(e) complete <<- TRUE)

  i <- 0
  fin <- function(e) {
    i <<- i + 1
    if (!identical(complete, TRUE)) {
      reg.finalizer(environment(), fin)
    }
  }
  fin()

  function() i
}

gcs <- count_gc()
```

